---
import { execSync } from 'node:child_process';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import astroPkg from 'astro/package.json';

// How the site is made, with the build stamping itself at the bottom. The
// weight table is written in after the build by tools/weigh.mjs, which
// replaces the marker below with what it measured.

let commit = '';
try {
  commit = execSync('git rev-parse --short HEAD', { encoding: 'utf8' }).trim();
} catch {
  commit = '';
}
const built = new Date();
const builtOn = built.toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
});
const repo = 'https://github.com/brettmboggs/brettboggs.dev';
---

<Base title="Colophon · Brett Boggs" description="How this site is made: the tools, the pipeline, and the weight of every page.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p>
    <h1>How this site is made.</h1>
    <p class="lede">The tools, the pipeline, and the weight of every page. Built by hand, hosted for nothing.</p>
  </header>

  <p class="stamp">
    <span>Build {commit || 'local'}</span>
    <span>{builtOn}</span>
    <span>Astro {astroPkg.version}</span>
    <a href={repo} rel="noopener">source</a>
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The stack</h2>
    <p>
      Astro, fully static. There is no framework in the page and no CSS framework under it: plain
      CSS with custom properties, and TypeScript written for the one page that needs it. GSAP drives
      the film scrub, and three.js turns the models. Fonts are self hosted: Fraunces for display,
      Instrument Sans for reading, Spline Sans Mono for labels. A GitHub Action builds every push
      and GitHub Pages serves it under a domain from Namecheap, which puts the monthly bill at zero.
    </p>

    <h2>The film</h2>
    <p>
      The Ridge was modelled and rendered in Blender with Cycles, 192 frames, on free CC0 assets
      from Poly Haven for the sky, the ground and the grass. A Node script runs the frames through
      sharp into WebP at two sizes with posters and a manifest. In the browser the frames decode into
      a canvas and the scroll position picks the frame, so the flyover runs at whatever speed you
      read. The last movement plants the station: the menu on the ridge is a pure function of how
      far the film has run, and its landmarks were measured off the frame in pixels. Choosing one is
      a camera dive, and the page you land on keeps the same crop underneath as a faint ghost.
    </p>

    <h2>The lab</h2>
    <p>
      Each experiment brings its own engine. The MacArthur is a three.js orbit viewer over models
      built from public survey data. The Field is a WebGPU compute shader with a spring for every
      blade. The keepsake is an SVG drawn from a seed, so the same address always draws the same
      field. Everything that moves has a still or a fallback for browsers and people who would
      rather it did not.
    </p>

    <h2>Type and motion</h2>
    <p>
      Fraunces does the art direction with its optical size, softness and wonk axes. Page heads
      interpolate those axes on a scroll timeline as they leave the screen, display type settling
      into text, with no script involved. Pages change under native cross-document view transitions.
      Every motion on the site is switched off for a reduced motion preference. One gotcha for
      anyone copying this: the default CSS minifier folds scroll timelines into the animation
      shorthand, which stable browsers reject, so the build minifies CSS with esbuild instead.
    </p>

    <h2>Offline</h2>
    <p>
      A service worker caches the core pages and posters at install, fetches navigations network
      first so a new deploy is never served stale, and serves hashed assets cache first. The site
      installs, and there is a page for when the signal goes. The film frames stay online only.
    </p>

    <h2>The store</h2>
    <p>
      The shelf is a static page with Stripe Elements in the browser. A Cloudflare Worker holds the
      catalogue, keeps the pictures in KV, and prices every order again from its own list, because
      nothing the page believes about money is trusted. It runs in test mode until it does not.
    </p>

    <h2>Machine readers</h2>
    <p>
      There is an <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>, a <a href="/resume.json">JSON Resume</a> that
      also renders the <a href="/resume/">resume page</a>, and schema.org data on the front page.
      Each page gets its own social card at build time, drawn in the site's type by satori and
      rasterised by sharp, from the same list the site uses for titles. The
      <a href="/log/">log</a> reads git during the build.
    </p>

    <h2>Dusk</h2>
    <p>
      After sunset the paper cools and the ink deepens on the reading pages. Sunset is guessed
      from your clock and the month, not from where you are. Add <code>?dusk=1</code> or
      <code>?dusk=0</code> to any address to choose for yourself; the choice stays on your device.
    </p>

    <h2>The weight</h2>
    <p>
      Measured after every build: the HTML, the CSS and the script each page loads, gzipped as a
      browser would fetch them. Pictures, film frames and models are not counted. They are most of
      what you see and none of what this table is about.
    </p>
    <div class="weigh"><!--weigh--></div>
  </div>

  <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Home</a>
</Base>

<style>
  .stamp {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.6rem;
    margin-top: 2.6rem;
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .stamp a {
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--rust);
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    color: var(--rust);
  }
  .weigh {
    margin-top: 1.6em;
  }
  .weigh :global(table) {
    display: block;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .weigh :global(th) {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    padding: 0 1.4rem 0.55rem 0;
  }
  .weigh :global(td) {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--paper-deep);
    padding: 0.5rem 1.4rem 0.5rem 0;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .weigh :global(td:not(:first-child)),
  .weigh :global(th:not(:first-child)) {
    text-align: right;
  }
  .weigh :global(th:last-child),
  .weigh :global(td:last-child) {
    padding-right: 0;
  }
  .weigh :global(td a) {
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .weigh :global(.weigh-note) {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
